{
 "id": "arc-length-sector-area",
 "title": "Arc length & sector area applications",
 "summary": "Use s = rθ and A = ½r²θ to solve problems about wheels, sprinklers and pendulums.",
 "objectives": [
  "Find arc length and sector area with the angle in radians",
  "Solve for the radius or the angle",
  "Relate angular and linear speed"
 ],
 "prerequisites": [
  "degree-radian-conversion"
 ],
 "lesson": [
  {
   "id": "als-b01",
   "type": "text",
   "md": "# Arc length and sector area\n\nWith the angle in **radians**, the length of an arc and the area of a slice of a circle come from two short formulas. They only work in radians, which is the main reason radians exist."
  },
  {
   "id": "als-b02",
   "type": "formulas",
   "title": "Formulas for a circle of radius $r$ (angles in radians)",
   "items": [
    {
     "name": "Arc length",
     "tex": "s=r\\theta",
     "when": "Distance along the circle.",
     "vars": [
      {
       "symbol": "s",
       "meaning": "arc length"
      },
      {
       "symbol": "r",
       "meaning": "radius"
      },
      {
       "symbol": "\\theta",
       "meaning": "central angle",
       "unit": "rad"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "name": "Sector area",
     "tex": "A=\\tfrac12 r^2\\theta",
     "when": "The area of the slice.",
     "vars": []
    },
    {
     "name": "Angular speed",
     "tex": "\\omega=\\dfrac{\\theta}{t}",
     "when": "Radians turned per unit of time.",
     "vars": []
    },
    {
     "name": "Linear speed",
     "tex": "v=r\\omega",
     "when": "Speed of a point on the rim.",
     "vars": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "als-b03",
   "type": "predict",
   "question": "A pizza has radius $8$ in. If the radius doubles to $16$ in and the slice angle stays the same, the crust along the edge of the slice…",
   "options": [
    {
     "md": "doubles",
     "correct": true,
     "why": "$s=r\\theta$ is proportional to $r$."
    },
    {
     "md": "is multiplied by $4$",
     "correct": false,
     "why": "That is what happens to the **area**, $\\tfrac12r^2\\theta$."
    },
    {
     "md": "stays the same",
     "correct": false,
     "why": "The angle is the same, but the arc is farther from the center."
    }
   ],
   "explanation": "Arc length grows like $r$; sector area grows like $r^2$."
  },
  {
   "id": "als-b04",
   "type": "plot",
   "title": "A sector with central angle $\\theta$",
   "xMin": -1.35,
